# Changelog (engine/v1/)

## Simulator GLB export

**What:** The Simulator can now export the loaded object as a self-contained `.glb` via `[E]` or
`ENGINE.Simulator.Download()`. A new `/* === GLB EXPORT === */` block in `Simulator.js` writes the
glTF 2.0 container by hand: one node per mesh (`node.matrix` = `CreateModelMatrix(mesh.transform)`,
geometry kept local), de-indexed triangles with flat normals, `faceTextureGroups` split into
primitives, CPU-baked triplanar UVs, CPU-ported decal grid/placement/surface-projection, and baked
texture canvases re-encoded to PNG with an alpha scan driving `alphaMode`.

`exportMeshList` mirrors the renderer's mode rules per object type — terrain by mesh mode, obstacles
by record mode, entity parts unfiltered — so void and invisible meshes never reach the writer.

Decals are lifted off their host along `decalSurfaceNormal`, the analytic surface normal, replacing
`POLYGON_OFFSET_FILL`, which glTF has no equivalent for. The lift is uniform across every decal on a
host — the deepest chord dip among them plus an epsilon — with `index * decalLayerStep` added on top
so authored order decides layering rather than chord depth. `buildDecalGrid` raises grid resolution
from 12 up to `decalMaxSegments` until the measured dip falls under `decalDipTarget`. That same
normal is what the decal emits, so winding no longer affects the result and a mirrored decal
(negative `localTransform.scale`) exports facing outward.

**Where:** [Simulator.js](engine/v1/handlers/game/Simulator.js) — new export block `:360-807`
(constants, `exportMeshList`, `meshPrimitiveSpans`, `buildMeshVertices`, `decalSurfaceNormal`,
`forEachDecalCell`, `buildDecalGrid`, `buildDecalVertices`, `projectDecalPoint`,
`encodeTextureImage`, the `push*`/`writeGlbBinary` writer, `Download` `:743-807`);
`simulatorRuntime.loadedId` `:36`; HUD row `:80` and control bar `:86`;
`[E]` in `HandleSimulatorInput` `:819`; export list `:866`.
[ini.js](engine/v1/core/ini.js) — `Download` imported `:16` and exposed on `ENGINE.Simulator` `:65`.

## Jump veto measured along the contact normal

**What:** `ResolveGrounded`'s jump veto now tests the velocity component along the contact's own up
(`DotVector3(entity.velocity, entity.alignedUp)`) instead of world `velocity.y`. Landing on an
inclined walkable surface with forward momentum leaves velocity entirely tangent to that surface but
with a positive world y, which previously vetoed grounding and locked the player out of jumping.

**Where:** [Correction.js](engine/v1/physics/Correction.js) — `ResolveGrounded`.
